“What?” He was dumbfounded. “What are you saying?” “You love this child. Am I right?”
Not that question. Not again! “Love? Is it always love that ﬁlls your little head, Anna? Not everyone sees the world in pastel colors, you know. Listen, he likes the school, he asked me to go, and I think it will be good for him. That’s it.”
“Answer my question.”
Christopher let out a little puﬀ of air from his parted lips. “You know very well that I ﬁnd your family very likable,” he ﬁnally admitted.
“Likable?” She laughed bitterly, shaking her head. “You haven’t answered, Christopher. Let me be crystal clear. If I rebel against you—if, for example, I say to your friend Leopold DeMercy and your business associates what I think of them, or if Thursday night at the Mortimers’ I don’t wear the dress that you have chosen for me—well, if I don’t do all the things that you ask”—she barely blushed—“you would take it out on me with my family members, wouldn’t you? So you said.”
